WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
So far we have covered how to recognize cardiac arrest and how to perform high-quality CPR on your own.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Now we are going to add a powerful tool to our rescue, the AED or automated external defibrillator.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.000
An AED is a portable device that can actually analyze a heart's rhythm and, if necessary,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.000
deliver a shock to help restore a normal heartbeat.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.000
It doesn't just shock, however. The AED will also guide you through chest compressions and breaths

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.000
while turning on a metronome to help you maintain rhythm.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Think of it as a smart assistant that talks you through every single step with clear and simple voice prompts.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
They are designed to be simple for non-medical bystanders to use.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.000
When you open an AED, you will usually find a small kit inside to help you prepare the patient.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
This typically includes gloves for your safety, scissors to cut through clothing and expose the bare chest,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
and a small towel to wipe away any sweat or water.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.000
You might even find a razor.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
If a person has a very hairy chest, the pads might not stick properly,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
so you can use the razor to quickly shave just the spots where the pads need to go.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Using the AED is as simple as following these steps.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.000
First, turn it on and apply the pads to the bare chest as soon as it is available.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
As soon as it is turned on, the AED will begin giving you audible instructions.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Second, check your pad placement against the pictures provided on the pads themselves.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Third, listen carefully to the voice prompts.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
If the device says shock advised, make sure no one is touching the person.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Shout clear, loudly, and look to ensure everyone is back before you press the shock button.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.000
The AED will never shock on its own.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Your instructor is going to demonstrate how to use the Trainer AED now,

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000
and then you'll have a chance to try it yourself and ask any questions.

